{"ts": 0.148237, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97m1.2 — keyboard habits\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nFour keys make the shell comfortable. Try each one now.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[95mTab\u001b[0m       bash finishes a word. Type \u001b[92mecho p\u001b[0m, press \u001b[95mTab\u001b[0m:\r\n            it becomes \u001b[92mecho poem.txt\u001b[0m. Several matches? The first\r\n            \u001b[95mTab\u001b[0m fills in the shared part; \u001b[95mTab\u001b[0m again lists them.\r\n            (Other shells and setups differ.)\r\n  \u001b[95m↑\u001b[0m         brings back the last command. Enter runs it again.\r\n  \u001b[95mCtrl+L\u001b[0m    clears the screen; the command \u001b[92mclear\u001b[0m does the same.\r\n            Type \u001b[92mshow\u001b[0m to bring this lesson back afterwards.\r\n  \u001b[95mCtrl+C\u001b[0m    the escape hatch. If anything feels stuck, press it\r\n            and you get a fresh prompt.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Try each key, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 787}
